result_payload.proof_nl:
Let n>1 and let r be real with 0<=r<1. Define m=⌊nr⌋. Then m<=nr<m+1, so dividing by n>0 gives m/n<=r. Now fix any k with 1<=k<=n. Since k>0, multiplying preserves the inequality: k(m/n)<=kr. The floor function is nondecreasing, hence ⌊k(m/n)⌋<=⌊kr⌋.

Also, since m/n is a rational number with n(m/n)=m, we have ⌊n(m/n)⌋=m=⌊nr⌋.

Therefore
f_n(r)=⌊nr⌋-\sum_{k=1}^n \frac{⌊kr⌋}{k}
<= m-\sum_{k=1}^n \frac{⌊k(m/n)⌋}{k}
=⌊n(m/n)⌋-\sum_{k=1}^n \frac{⌊k(m/n)⌋}{k}
=f_n(m/n).

This is exactly the desired inequality.
